  "slug": "models-learning-5db9",
  "summary": "Recent preprints probe how torsion, coordinate, and electron-density parametrizations shape learned biomolecular models; all results are unverified.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is coupling learned models to an explicit physical description of biomolecules. EscalNet learns an isotropic representation space from biomolecular trajectories using Fourier transform analysis and derives saliency-based structural determinants. A torsion-space diffusion model generates protein backbones with differentiable forward kinematics and Rg-based geometric refinement. A voxel study compares atom-type, electron density, and density-gradient inputs for 3D CNNs on PDBbind affinity and QM9 property prediction, making representation choice the object of study. FoldSAE applies sparse autoencoders to RFdiffusion internal representations to find secondary-structure features, with a tunable steering mechanism. What is new is attention to the parametrization of inputs or coordinates rather than architecture alone. All reported results are unverified preprint claims.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-28"
}
